{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"bb5820539e1751cd","name":"card_event_front_and_homeland","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Germany/Homefront/events/card_event_front_and_homeland","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FRONT AND HOMELAND","text":"Draw from your deck on turn 4.\r\nReduce the cost of German cards with odd cost in your deck by 1.","flavorText":"Throughout WWII, German propaganda worked tirelessly to link the front lines with the civilian population. The concept of “Front und Heimat” (e. front and homeland) emphasized unity between soldiers and those supporting the war effort at home. Soldiers were portrayed as selfless defenders of the nation, while civilians, especially women, were called to maintain production, morale and to support rationing. The regime promoted this connection through newspapers, radio, and cinema, depicting shared sacrifice and mutual reliance. Packages from home, letters, and news from the front helped sustain this illusion. But as the war progressed, the hardships grew both at home and on the front. While German industry shifted to total war production by 1943 under Albert Speer, cities suffered devastating Allied bombing campaigns. Civilian life grew harsher, with widespread shortages and destruction. On the front, soldiers endured brutal winters, extended deployments, and mounting casualties. Still, the idea of “Front und Heimat” remained central to Nazi propaganda, used to justify hardship and rally support long after military momentum had turned against Germany.","faction":"Germany","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_front_and_homeland","title":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_front_and_homeland_title","SourceString":"FRONT AND HOMELAND","LocalizedString":"FRONT AND HOMELAND"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Germany","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_front_and_homeland_text","SourceString":"Draw from your deck on turn 4.\r\nReduce the cost of German cards with odd cost in your deck by 1.","LocalizedString":"Draw from your deck on turn 4.\r\nReduce the cost of German cards with odd cost in your deck by 1."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_front_and_homeland_history","SourceString":"Throughout WWII, German propaganda worked tirelessly to link the front lines with the civilian population. The concept of “Front und Heimat” (e. front and homeland) emphasized unity between soldiers and those supporting the war effort at home. Soldiers were portrayed as selfless defenders of the nation, while civilians, especially women, were called to maintain production, morale and to support rationing. The regime promoted this connection through newspapers, radio, and cinema, depicting shared sacrifice and mutual reliance. Packages from home, letters, and news from the front helped sustain this illusion. But as the war progressed, the hardships grew both at home and on the front. While German industry shifted to total war production by 1943 under Albert Speer, cities suffered devastating Allied bombing campaigns. Civilian life grew harsher, with widespread shortages and destruction. On the front, soldiers endured brutal winters, extended deployments, and mounting casualties. Still, the idea of “Front und Heimat” remained central to Nazi propaganda, used to justify hardship and rally support long after military momentum had turned against Germany.","LocalizedString":"Throughout WWII, German propaganda worked tirelessly to link the front lines with the civilian population. The concept of “Front und Heimat” (e. front and homeland) emphasized unity between soldiers and those supporting the war effort at home. Soldiers were portrayed as selfless defenders of the nation, while civilians, especially women, were called to maintain production, morale and to support rationing. The regime promoted this connection through newspapers, radio, and cinema, depicting shared sacrifice and mutual reliance. Packages from home, letters, and news from the front helped sustain this illusion. But as the war progressed, the hardships grew both at home and on the front. While German industry shifted to total war production by 1943 under Albert Speer, cities suffered devastating Allied bombing campaigns. Civilian life grew harsher, with widespread shortages and destruction. On the front, soldiers endured brutal winters, extended deployments, and mounting casualties. Still, the idea of “Front und Heimat” remained central to Nazi propaganda, used to justify hardship and rally support long after military momentum had turned against Germany."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Germany/10_Homefront/T_front_and_homeland.T_front_and_homeland","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/FRONT_AND_HOMELAND.FRONT_AND_HOMELAND","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":325.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_germany","Key":"card_event_front_and_homeland_photo","SourceString":"German infantry and horse-drawn transports in harsh winter conditions on the Eastern Front, 1941.","LocalizedString":"German infantry and horse-drawn transports in harsh winter conditions on the Eastern Front, 1941."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""}},"variables":[{"name":"cardsInDeck","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"evenCount","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"topCardId","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"Execu